# IRC from the VERIFIED TS (COMPUTATIONAL.md C8 item 1).
#
# Purpose: show that the single imaginary mode at -576.3 cm-1 IS the reaction
# coordinate -- that it connects the reactant pre-complex to the product --
# rather than trusting a mode tally on a floppy 140-atom host. It also produces
# the product endpoint, which the reverse rate needs.
#
# Geometry is ../ts/verify_exact_hessian/job.xyz (md5 fb47acb010589f690425efa03e53a441),
# the geometry whose exact NumFreq gave one imaginary mode. It is NOT
# ../ts/job.xyz, which a crashed OptTS attempt overwrote (O136-C129 2.596 vs the
# verified 1.820 A).
#
# Same method and solvent as the geometry/frequency tier, which the manual
# requires for an IRC ("The same method and basis set as used for optimization
# and frequency calculation should be used"). 20-atom QM region, GFNFF topology,
# charge 0.
#
# InitHess is DELIBERATELY left at ORCA's default. A standalone IRC job computes
# a fresh numerical Hessian at the start, which is what we want. We do NOT read
# ../ts/verify_exact_hessian/job.hess: that file carries an empty active-region
# record ($act_atom 0) and crashed ORCA in LEANSCF when the OptTS attempt tried
# `inhess Read` on it (COMPUTATIONAL.md 2026-09-19).
! QM/XTB r2SCAN-3c ddCOSMO(Water) IRC TightSCF

%QMMM
QMATOMS
	{61 64 120 121 122 124 125 126 128 129 130 131 132 133 134 135 136 137 138 139}
END
Charge_total 0
Mult_total 1
AutoFF_QM2_Method GFNFF
END

%irc
  MaxIter 20
  Direction both
  # The relay, watched as it moves: the forming C-O bond and the two protons.
  # Indices are 0-based, so 135 = atom 136 (O136), 128 = atom 129 (C129).
  Monitor_Internals
    {B 135 128}
    {B 129 130}
    {B 131 130}
  end
END

%output jsonpropfile true end

%maxcore 4000

%pal
   nprocs 8
end

* xyzfile 0 1 job.xyz
